/// A user's Gmail drafts created for an assistant, as the native tool
/// `GmailDraft`, answered by `api-request` calls against the `oauth_provider`
/// the instance names, stamped with the calling conversation's owner. The
/// user's token never reaches this component; `api-request` attaches it
/// host-side. Every call is nested under a request: an instance whose clauses
/// say `user = "*"` cannot call on its own account, so a caller with no user
/// of its own is answered `denied`. Its calls are Gmail's `drafts.create` by
/// upload, under the Gmail compose scope set, and, for a reply, `messages.get`
/// of the original's headers first, under the Gmail read scope set. The
/// upload's body is the message the component builds — the text, then one
/// part per file the call attached — and, for a reply, the upload's metadata
/// names the original's thread. No call sends mail.
///
/// Every capability is load-bearing: `config` names the provider and its
/// host, without which no call can be addressed; `store` carries each
/// request's state between the activation that issues its nested call and the
/// one that receives the answer; `tools` is the authority to call
/// `api-request`; `provides` is what makes the tool exist; `blobs` opens the
/// request's files and builds the message the upload sends; `log` records
/// each step's tool, status and size — never a body, a header or a query.
///
/// A draft takes at most 5 files of at most 10 MiB together, refused before
/// any call; the widest message built from them is under 14.5 MB, so the four
/// bodies the kit builds in one activation stay inside the host's 64 MiB of
/// blob writes an activation. A file is charged to the blob pool once, when
/// it was written: the request's row on `brenn:tool-requests/gmail-compose`
/// holds every file the call attached until the bus's collection after the
/// row leaves that window, whether the call was refused or not. The built
/// message is new bytes, named by the nested call's row on
/// `brenn:tools/api-request` until the collection after that row leaves its
/// window; nothing is built for a refused call. Size `blob_budget_bytes` and
/// `max_blob_bytes` as the guide says. Measured on the real host, the
/// costliest build at the file bound completes on about 0.22 of its fuel
/// grant, inside the 19/28 margin a component's bounds are held to.
///
/// The body's bound, 20,000 bytes, is kept by choice: the body rides the
/// request's in-flight row, which a late answer's settle decodes on the
/// envelope term alone. Five late answers to the widest reply settled in one
/// activation, with the costliest body, were measured at 0.348 of their
/// grant for the upload and 0.692 for the read of the original, which also
/// parses the arguments again; by the measured slopes the read's meets the
/// margin at a body of about 19,600 bytes, so at this bound it is over the
/// margin, though inside the grant.
///
/// The instance's `config = { ... };` map is flat: `provider` is the
/// `oauth_provider` name; `gmail_host` is the `host[:port]` Gmail is called
/// on, which `api-request` matches against that provider's `api_hosts` and
/// the instance's clauses must admit. A missing or malformed key fails every
/// activation, naming the key.
///
/// Size the instance's result inbox with a `channel at
/// "brenn:tool-results/<slug>"` block: each live request has at most one
/// nested call outstanding, so the window must hold Σ max_concurrency results,
/// twice over for a re-delivered result — `retain_depth ≥ 2 × Σ`, rounded up
/// to a power of two. This class's Σ is 2, which the family default of 16
/// holds; the block in the guide restates it and sets `noise = alarm`.
///
/// Every nested answer crosses the bus as a tool result, so Google's body — a
/// JSON string inside `api-request`'s value, inside the result envelope — must
/// fit `[messaging] max_body_bytes` (64 KiB default). Either raise that cap to
/// carry the OAuth provider's `max_response_bytes` (256 KiB default) with a
/// margin for escaping, `messaging { max_body_bytes = 393216; }`, or lower
/// `max_response_bytes` to fit the default cap, at the cost of most HTML mail.
/// Half again covers message bodies, which are base64url and do not grow when
/// escaped; sender-written headers dense in quotes or backslashes escape to
/// about twice their size, so such a read can pass the cap below the read
/// limit.
/// An answer over the bus cap never reaches this component as Google's answer;
/// the tool treats that refusal as it treats an answer cut at the read limit.
component GoogleGmailCompose {
  abi = processor;
  requires = [log, store, config, tools, provides, blobs];
  in tool-results;
  in tool-requests;
  provide gmail-draft {
    mcp_name = "GmailDraft";
    description = "Create a draft in the user's Gmail. The draft is saved in Gmail's Drafts for the user to review and send from Gmail; no tool sends it. `to` and `cc` are the recipients as an email's header writes them (`Alice Example <alice@example.com>, bob@example.com`), each 1 to 980 printable ASCII characters: a display name in another script is left out or written in ASCII. `subject` is at most 500 characters with no control character or line break, and is required unless `reply_to_id` is given. `body` is the message's plain text, at most 20000 characters and about 20000 bytes of UTF-8 text, where a line break counts as two; a longer body is refused. Files travel as the call's `attachments`, at most 5 files of at most 10485760 bytes together: each is attached under the name the call gives it, which is the file name the recipient sees, so name it as a file is named (`report.pdf`), and carries the content type it has; more files or more bytes are refused. `reply_to_id` (a message `id` from `GmailSearch` or `GmailRead`) makes the draft a reply in that message's thread: the original is read first, which needs the Gmail read scope set, and the draft carries the original's `Message-ID` as `In-Reply-To` and `References`. Without `subject`, a reply's subject is the original's with `Re: ` before it (kept as is when it already starts with `Re:`), and an original whose subject is over 1000 bytes is refused: give `subject`. Gmail files a draft in the thread only when its subject matches the thread's and it carries the original's `Message-ID`, so a `subject` given for a reply is the thread's. Answers `draft_id`, `message_id` and `thread_id` (when Google gives it); for a reply, also `in_reply_to` (the `Message-ID` the draft answers, absent when the original has no usable one), `subject` when it was derived, and `in_thread: false` when Gmail filed the draft outside the original's thread: the draft exists as a new conversation, so tell the user rather than drafting again; nothing the caller wrote is echoed. A refusal saying the draft may have been created means Google may have saved it before its answer was lost: look for it with `GmailSearch` (`q: in:drafts`) before asking again, or the user has two. `account` is needed only when the user has several Google accounts connected: the refusal lists them. An answer `{\"error\": \"credential_invalid\", …}` means this user has no usable Google credential: run `oauth-connect` for the provider with the Gmail compose scope set the operator named and relay its steps, then `oauth-accounts` confirms. Gmail endpoints these tools do not cover (profile, history) and Calendar's colors and settings are listed with `api-request` examples in `docs/providers/google.md`.";
    input_schema = """{ "type": "object", "properties": { "to": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[\\x20-\\x7e]{1,980}$" }, "cc": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[\\x20-\\x7e]{1,980}$" }, "subject": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 500, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "body": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 20000, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" }, "reply_to_id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}$" }, "account": { "type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 320, "pattern": "[^\\s\\u0085]" } }, "required": ["to", "body"], "additionalProperties": false }""";
    accepts_attachments = true;
    max_concurrency = 2;
  }
}
